Late to the thread here, but I'd add to John's great answer which addresses (in part) how short you can make your intervals, the importance of assessing closure for your species and study area.  If the intervals are long enough for individuals to enter or leave the area without being accounted for by covariates, then you may be violating the closure assumption.
-Alex

